 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.1,
   "dur": 4.688,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "Where does your breath go? Not into a bag. Into these."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 5.5,
   "dur": 10.537,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "Below your windpipe, the airways fork, again and again, up to about twenty-three times. At the tips hang about half a billion sacs. Each is about a fifth of a millimetre."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 17.1,
   "dur": 10.226,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "For centuries, physicians taught the lung was airy flesh, there to cool the heart. In sixteen sixty-one, Marcello Malpighi put a frog's lung under a microscope: bubbles."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 28.3,
   "dur": 13.668,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "Each sac is wrapped in capillaries. At its thinnest, the wall between air and blood is about half a micrometre: far thinner than a red cell. Oxygen slips across, no pump needed. Thicken that wall, and breathing gets harder."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 44.3,
   "dur": 8.237,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "Unfold every sac flat, and they'd cover at least seventy square metres. At least a third of a tennis court, folded inside your chest."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 55.9,
   "dur": 4.497,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "So your lungs aren't a bag. They're a sponge that spreads air thin."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 61.1,
   "dur": 5.725,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "Meanwhile, as you watched, air went in and out of you. About six litres of it."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 67.9,
   "dur": 4.355,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next: a cell that leaves your blood. It squeezes through a wall."
  }
 ]
}